From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maicon Alves <alvesmaiconn@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 01:03:39 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 33/44] client: the player in water moves as in Minecraft
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Water only braked the player: under water the walk still accelerated at
30 m/s² toward its speed, and the player counted as in a liquid only with
the feet 0.5 under the surface, out again at 0.1, so jump held at the
surface left the water at every bob and the air accelerated with nothing
to brake it. Walking and running on the water went at 3.4 and 5.8 m/s,
under water at 2.7 (Minecraft: 2.3 at the surface, 1.95 under water).

Nodes of the group water now move the player as Minecraft 26.3's
LivingEntity.travelInWater, Player.travel and LocalPlayer.aiStep do. The
player is in the water while any part of the box is under its surface
(as drawn, flowing water by its level). Each tick (20 Hz) the input
pushes 0.02, jump 0.04 up, sneak 0.04 down, sinking 0.005, then 80 % of
the speed is kept. This is the same approach in continuous time, so the
speeds and how fast they come are the same at any frame rate. Sneaking
moves at 30 %; speed overrides only slow down here (an item in use), as
Minecraft's speed doesn't swim faster; jump is a ground jump only where
the water is at most 0.4 deep.

The new physics override swim is Minecraft's swimming, for the game to
set (goodluckOS sets it while the player runs with the head under water):
no sinking, 90 % of the speed kept, and up or down toward where the
player looks.

Pushing on a wall in the water lifts the player at 0.3 a tick unless
something is 0.6 above it, so a ledge is climbed out of the water as in
Minecraft. Airborne between bobs at the surface, the player steers with
Minecraft's air (0.02 a tick, 91 % kept) until it touches the ground:
with the engine's air control, much stronger, the surface still went at
3.8 m/s.

Flowing water pushes the player as Minecraft's FlowingFluid.getFlow and
applyCurrentTo do: 0.014 a tick along the average flow of the nodes it is
in (weaker in water shallower than 0.4), at least 0.0045 on a player at
rest; a fall of flowing water along a wall pushes down.

Checked in the native build with keys pressed by xdotool in a deep pool,
at 60 and 15 FPS (Minecraft in brackets, m/s): walking under water 1.94
(1.95), swimming 3.88 (3.90), swimming and looking 45° down 3.89 and
-4.46 (3.90, -4.46), up 3.89 and +3.40 (3.90, +3.40), jump held +3.47
(+3.48), sneak -4.50 (-4.48), idle -0.50 (-0.50), jump and forward at
the surface 2.57 (2.33); at 15 FPS within 2 %. On a stream: 0.84 m/s
downstream at rest, 1.45 walking upstream (1.95 in still water).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

